# Day 09: Advanced PowerShell Scripting

## Overview

On Day 09 of my PowerShell 10 Days: Zero to Hero challenge, I focused on Advanced PowerShell Scripting using Windows Server 2022.

The goal of this practical session was to understand how PowerShell scripting can be structured using variables, functions, loops, conditional logic, and error handling to automate administrative tasks and improve script reliability.

## Key Learning Areas

- PowerShell Variables
- Functions and Reusable Commands
- ForEach Loops
- For Loops
- While Loops
- Conditional Logic (If/Else)
- Error Handling (Try/Catch/Finally)
- ErrorAction and Exception Handling

## Hands-on Practical Exercises

### Lab 1 – Variables

Use variables to store and retrieve server information.

```powershell
$ServerName = $env:COMPUTERNAME
$Admin = "System Administrator"
$OS = (Get-CimInstance Win32_OperatingSystem).Caption

$ServerName
$Admin
$OS

Write-Host "Server Name: $ServerName"
Write-Host "Administrator: $Admin"
Write-Host "Operating System: $OS"
```

### Lab 2 – Functions

Create reusable PowerShell functions to retrieve server information and check WinRM service status.

```powershell
function Get-ServerInfo {
    Get-CimInstance Win32_OperatingSystem |
    Select-Object CSName, Caption, Version
}

Get-ServerInfo
```

Create a function to check WinRM:

```powershell
function Get-WinRMStatus {
    Get-Service WinRM |
    Select-Object Name, Status, StartType
}

Get-WinRMStatus
```

### Lab 3 – Loops

#### ForEach Loop – Service Monitoring

Check multiple services using a loop.

```powershell
$Services = @("WinRM", "DNS", "NTDS", "LanmanServer")

foreach ($ServiceName in $Services) {
    Get-Service -Name $ServiceName |
    Select-Object Name, Status, StartType
}
```

#### For Loop – Numbered Iteration

```powershell
for ($i = 1; $i -le 5; $i++) {
    Write-Host "Monitoring Check Number: $i"
}
```

#### While Loop – Repeated Status Checks

```powershell
$count = 1

while ($count -le 3) {
    $Service = Get-Service WinRM

    Write-Host "Check $count - WinRM Status: $($Service.Status)"

    $count++
}
```

#### ForEach Loop with Conditional Logic

```powershell
$Services = @("WinRM", "DNS", "NTDS", "LanmanServer")

foreach ($ServiceName in $Services) {

    $Service = Get-Service -Name $ServiceName

    if ($Service.Status -eq "Running") {
        Write-Host "$ServiceName is RUNNING" -ForegroundColor Green
    }
    else {
        Write-Host "$ServiceName is NOT RUNNING" -ForegroundColor Yellow
    }
}
```

### Lab 4 – Error Handling

#### Try and Catch

```powershell
try {
    Get-Service -Name "FakeService" -ErrorAction Stop
}
catch {
    Write-Host "Error detected: Service was not found." -ForegroundColor Red
}
```

#### Try, Catch and Finally

```powershell
try {
    Write-Host "Checking WinRM service..." -ForegroundColor Cyan

    Get-Service -Name WinRM -ErrorAction Stop
}
catch {
    Write-Host "An error occurred during the check." -ForegroundColor Red
}
finally {
    Write-Host "Service check completed." -ForegroundColor Yellow
}
```

#### Advanced Service Monitoring with Error Handling

```powershell
$Services = @("WinRM", "DNS", "NTDS", "FakeService")

foreach ($ServiceName in $Services) {

    try {
        $Service = Get-Service -Name $ServiceName -ErrorAction Stop

        Write-Host "$ServiceName : $($Service.Status)" -ForegroundColor Green
    }
    catch {
        Write-Host "$ServiceName : Service not found or error occurred." -ForegroundColor Red
    }
}
```

#### Detailed Error Information

```powershell
try {
    Get-Service -Name "FakeService" -ErrorAction Stop
}
catch {
    Write-Host "Error Message:" -ForegroundColor Red
    Write-Host $_.Exception.Message

    Write-Host "Error Type:" -ForegroundColor Yellow
    Write-Host $_.Exception.GetType().Name
}
finally {
    Write-Host "Error handling lab completed." -ForegroundColor Cyan
}
```

